Counting wrapper (for the demonstration, not part of the solutions):
```python
class Counted:
    eq_calls = hash_calls = lt_calls = 0
    def __init__(self, v):
        self.v = v
    def __eq__(self, other):
        Counted.eq_calls += 1
        return self.v == other.v
    def __hash__(self):
        Counted.hash_calls += 1
        return hash(self.v)
    def __lt__(self, other):
        Counted.lt_calls += 1
        return self.v < other.v
```
With 200 unique Counted items: has_duplicates_quadratic makes exactly 200*199/2 = 19,900 __eq__ calls; has_duplicates makes 200 __hash__ calls and 0 __eq__ calls (equality is consulted only when full hashes collide); has_duplicates_sorted makes about 1,270 __lt__ calls on shuffled input (roughly n log2 n).
